<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ibak Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1495">1495</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Ibaq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ibak Khan was a Shaybanid ruler of the Tyumen Khanate in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, whose people paid tribute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>. In 1481, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-stand-on-the-ugra-river">Great Stand on the Ugra River</a>, he attacked the camp of Ahmad Khan of the Great Horde and killed him. He claimed leadership among the Jochid khans and corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibak-khan.jpg" alt="Ibak Khan" width="320" height="561" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Russian manuscript illuminators, 1560-1570s Facial Chronicle (Illustrated Chronicle of Ivan the Terrible) (... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A6%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%8C_%D0%98%D0%B1%D0%B0%D0%BA_%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%B8%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%B5%D1%82_%D1%85%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%90%D1%85%D0%BC%D0%B0%D1%82%D0%B0.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
